From deepseek

Pool sizing logic: Your pool_find_size() checks if a slab size falls within min_mcache_size and max_mcache_size, but you never actually use the pool's mcache_count field, and pools don't track which mcaches belong to them—they just serve as a lookup range. This works but means pools are somewhat underutilized; you could instead store an array of mcache indices or pointers to make iteration faster.

Mcache creation: In slab_allocator_create_mcache(), you call pool_find_size() and then slab_allocator_instance_create_new_pool() if not found—but pool_find_size() itself calls slab_allocator_get_instance(), and then you call it again immediately after, so you're retrieving the instance twice. You could pass the instance pointer to avoid duplicate lookups.

Memory allocation for mcaches: You allocate slab_allocator_pool and slab_allocator_mcache tables using aligned_alloc() for each instance, but you never check if the tables are full—you assume SLAB_ALLOCATOR_POOL_TABLE_COUNT and SLAB_ALLOCATOR_MCACHE_TABLE_COUNT are sufficient. If you exceed these, you'll overflow. Consider dynamic growth or at least bounds checking.

mcache_extend_allocation(): This is interesting—you extend an existing mcache when it runs out of slabs, but you don't check if the new memory is contiguous with the old. You set mcache->last_slab_end = ptr + (slabs_capacity * slab_size) correctly, but in the extension, you only update num_pages in the handle, not the page_id—that's correct because the new pages are from the same mem_block (since you request from the same mem_id), so the page_id remains the starting page of the entire mcache. However, your free-list building in mcache_extend_allocation() assumes the new memory starts immediately after last_slab_end, but if the memory manager returns non-contiguous pages within the same block (due to fragmentation), your last_slab_end won't match the new address, and you'll have a gap. You already compute mem_is_contiguous but don't actually use it—you set ptr = address only if not contiguous, but you still build the free list from ptr onward. However, you don't link the old free list to the new free list—you overwrite mcache->slabs_free_list = (struct flist*) ptr;, which discards any existing free slabs from the old allocation. That's a bug: if there were free slabs in the old region, they become unreachable. You should either keep the old free list and append the new slabs to the end, or merge them correctly.

Free-list ordering: In mcache_free_slab(), you insert freed slabs in sorted order by address (using pointer comparison). This is good for coalescing but means your free list is always sorted, which makes allocation O(1) (just take the head) but free O(n) in the worst case (to find the insertion point). Since you always allocate from the head, you could instead use a LIFO (push to head) for O(1) free and allocation, but that would fragment slabs over time. Your sorted approach is fine for cache locality but be aware of the O(n) free cost.

mcache_alloc_slab(): You check mcache->slabs_count >= mcache->slabs_capacity to decide if you need to extend. However, slabs_count counts allocated slabs, and slabs_capacity counts total slabs. When you free a slab, slabs_count decrements, so you could have slabs_count < slabs_capacity but still have no free slabs if all are allocated—but your free list would be NULL in that case. You currently don't check mcache->slabs_free_list == NULL before extending, so if all slabs are allocated (free list empty) but slabs_count < slabs_capacity, you won't extend and will dereference NULL. You should check both conditions: if(mcache->slabs_free_list == NULL || mcache->slabs_count >= mcache->slabs_capacity).

slab_allocator_alloc(): You create a new mcache for every unique slab size. That's fine but could lead to many mcaches if you allocate many different sizes (e.g., 86, 162, 196). Consider rounding up to the next power-of-two or a predefined size class to reduce fragmentation and reuse.

Memory poisoning: You use memset(ptr, 0x0, requested_num_pages * PAGE_SIZE) in slab_allocator_create_mcache() and mcache_extend_allocation(), and you also zero out freed slabs in mcache_free_slab(). That's good for debugging but adds overhead—consider making it optional with a debug flag.

Type safety: You pass type_size and num_elems separately, but slab_size = num_elems * type_size. If type_size is not a multiple of the alignment requirement (e.g., 8-byte alignment for pointers), your slabs may be misaligned. You should round slab_size up to the required alignment for the type.

Instance table growth: Your slab_allocator_table is allocated with a fixed size (STACK_ALLOCATOR_INSTANCE_INITIAL_TABLE_COUNT), but you never reallocate if instance_count exceeds that—you'll overflow. Add bounds checking or dynamic growth.